module Core

import Native

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Hexadecimal - the digit in both directions, a run of bytes, and the formatter over one.
   ==========================================================================================

   Grouped here rather than spread across `Text.yana`, `Show.yana` and `Read.yana`, and that is the
   precedent this directory sets: a *notation* is one subject, so the unit predicate that reads a
   digit, the arithmetic that writes one, and the `Show` instance that writes a whole run of them
   belong beside each other rather than one to each of the three files a text library would otherwise
   grow without limit. `Core/Float/` is the same division made for the same reason a milestone
   earlier.

   `pushHex` is the half that is split by target, so it is in `Hex.native.yana` and `Hex.js.yana` -
   natively a vector loop into the string's own bytes, on JS a `pushUnit` pair per byte, and those
   two have nothing in common (Analysis-Modules.md §2.5). Everything else here is one body.
-}

{-
   A hexadecimal digit's value, or nothing for a unit that is not one.

   **The four-bit refinement is the whole of why this can be a `Maybe`.** A nibble has sixteen
   values and `@bits(4) U32`'s natural storage has 256, so `ReprTable::intNiche` donates
   `[16, 256)` and the folded `Maybe(HexDigit)` is one byte with `Nothing` written as 16 - no tag
   word, no second register, and a `Just` that is the digit itself. `doc/spec/repr.md` states the
   donation and `Native/Run.yana`'s `Count` is the same refinement used for the same reason.

   So the sentinel did not go away, it stopped being the *caller's* to know: what `-1` used to say
   in the return type this says in the constructor, and the number that stands for absence is the
   layout's business rather than a value in the digit's own range that every reader has to remember
   to test for. `test/resolve/Niche.yana` is where the donation is asserted, in the layout dump and
   behaviourally both.

   `@bits(4)` and not sixteen enum constructors: a refinement never participates in dispatch or in
   pattern matching, so a `Just(v)` binds an ordinary `U32` and arithmetic on it needs no instance of
   its own. That is what keeps `hexValue(u)` composable with the shifts a hexadecimal reader is
   written out of.

   **The layout is free and so, now, is the code.** Over a 64 KiB corpus of five-sixths hexadecimal
   digits at v2, one call per unit, each answer tested and contributed: the sentinel form is 4.58 ns
   per unit and this one is **4.36**, which is 0.95x over three runs. The `Maybe` is not a tax; it is
   marginally the cheaper of the two, because `cmp $0xf ; jbe` against the niche is the same shape as
   `test ; jl` against `-1` while the sentinel additionally holds `0xffffffff` in a register across
   the loop and sign-extends every `Just` on the way out.

   That is the second of two backend fixes and neither was about `Maybe`:

   - `promoteStackSlots` declined any slot whose accesses disagreed about the register type, and a
     folded `Maybe` is read once as the `Int` its discriminant test compares and again as the payload
     type - so every one of them was staying in memory. It holds the wider type and converts at each
     access now;
   - that conversion was a `bitcast`, which says nothing about the bits above the narrow value's
     width, so the mask `narrowedTo` puts on every load could never be folded away: `and $0xff`
     stood between the phi and the discriminant test on every read. A widening integer move is a
     zero extension now - the same instruction on this machine, and one `knownZeroBits` can see
     through. See `carriedInto` in compiler/lower/lower_promote.cpp, which states the argument.

   What is left is the refinement's own narrowing store, an `and $0xf` on each of the two `Just`
   arms. It is `@bits`' contract and the price of the niche, and removing it needs a range analysis
   rather than a known-bits one - the value is in range because the *branch* above it says the unit
   was between `0` and `9`, which is a fact `opt_range.cpp` deliberately does not carry. It is not
   costing anything against the sentinel, so it is a tidiness item rather than a performance one.
-}
pub alias HexDigit = @bits(4) U32

pub fn hexValue(unit: CodeUnit) -> Maybe(HexDigit)
  | "0"..="9" -> Just(truncate((unit - "0") :: U32))
  | "a"..="f" -> Just(truncate((unit - "a" + 10) :: U32))
  | "A"..="F" -> Just(truncate((unit - "A" + 10) :: U32))
  | _         -> Nothing

pub fn isHexDigit(unit: CodeUnit) -> Bool = hexValue(unit) is Just(_)

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Bytes as hexadecimal - and it is split, so the two halves are in the target files.
   ==========================================================================================

   The other direction of `hexValue` above, and the operation every digest, fingerprint and wire
   dump ends in: a run of bytes written out as two units apiece.

   **It was `Digest`'s own function**, which is where it was first wanted rather than where it
   belongs - nothing about writing a byte as two digits is a property of a message digest, and a
   second caller would have had to import one to get it. `Digest.hexOf` is now this one under the
   name it always had.

   `[U8]` and not a container class, and reached by the conversion at the call: a `Sha256Digest` is
   a `[U8 *32]` and becomes a slice where it is written, so this says nothing about widths and
   nothing about containers.

   **Lower case, with no argument to say otherwise.** Every protocol that compares digest text as a
   string - a `Content-MD5` header, a `.sha256` sidecar, git - writes it that way, and a case flag
   would be a branch inside the loop paid for by every caller that does not want one. The upper-case
   form is `toUpperAscii` of this, which is a second pass nobody has asked for yet.

   `pushHex` is the half that is split: natively it is a vector loop into the string's own bytes and
   on JS it is a `pushUnit` pair per byte, and those two have nothing in common (Analysis-Modules.md
   §2.5). `hexOf` is that function with the sink made for it, sized exactly, and is one body.
-}
pub fn hexOf(bytes: [U8]) -> String:
    let &text = String.ofCapacity(length(bytes) * 2)
    pushHex(bytes, text)
    return text

-- One nibble's digit - `0`-`9` then `a`-`f`, as arithmetic rather than as a table: the branch is
-- one `cmp` and a `cmov`, and a table would be a load and a bounds check the compiler cannot
-- remove. It is what the JS half and the native tail are written out of, and the native block form
-- is this same expression applied lanewise.
pub fn hexUnit(nibble: Int) -> CodeUnit = truncate(if nibble < 10 then "0" + nibble else nibble - 10 + "a")

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   `HexFormat` - a run of bytes in a format hole
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   `hexOf(digest)` answers a `String`, which is an allocation a format hole does not need: the hole
   already has a sink and a measured size, so what it wants is something that writes into one.
   That is what a `Show` instance is, and this is the record that carries the two settings a byte
   run has.

   So `print("sha256 = {hexBytes(digest)}\n")` formats a digest with no intermediate string at all,
   where `hexOf` builds one and copies it in. `hexOf` stays, because a program that wants the text
   as a value rather than as part of a bigger one should not have to make a sink to get it.

   **Named `hexBytes` and not `hex`.** An overload set admits one plain function per (name, arity)
   (doc/spec/overloading.md R1) and `Format/Number.yana` spends `hex` on an integer, which is the
   subject a program asks for by that name far more often. The two are different operations rather
   than one over two types - a number has a magnitude, a sign and a radix, and a byte run has an
   order and a separator - so a class unifying them would have nothing to put in its signature.

   `separator` is a `CodeUnit` with zero for none, for the reason `NumberFormat`'s `group` is:
   provenance is field-insensitive, so an aggregate argument reaching a record that holds a
   reference is read as a root of it.

   **The field is a `Flat(U8)` and the parameter a `'[U8]`, which are the same window said in the two
   positions' own spellings.** `[T]` in a field is the *owner* - `data Holder {items: Array(Int)}`
   holds its elements, which is Analysis-Borrows.md §2.5's rule - so a field that is a view has to
   name the descriptor type. On a parameter `[T]` already is the window, and the `'` there is the
   loan marker rather than part of the type: it is what tells a caller the result is rooted in this
   argument, which is exactly what a formatter hands back.
-}
pub data HexBytesStyle {
    upper: Bool = False,
    separator: CodeUnit = 0
}

{-
   A run of bytes under a style.

   **The subject is `[U8]`, and what the record stores is the window.** `[T]` in a field position is
   the *owner* - `data Holder {items: Array(Int)}` holds its elements, Analysis-Borrows.md §2.5's
   rule - but `Formatted`'s field is not `[T]`: it is `value: 'a`, a *declared borrow*, and a stored
   borrow of a container is that container's descriptor. So `Formatted(HexBytesStyle, [U8])` holds a
   base and a length whichever spelling the caller arrives with, and the one head below answers both.

   It used to be spelled `'[U8]`, naming the descriptor type directly, because the fold happened only
   where the borrow was *written* and not where it arrived by substitution. A capability is not a
   logical type, so that head named a type the ownership stage - where a window and the container it
   is over are one - does not have; the two stages could not both read this file.

   It is also why `Core/Format.yana`'s `showWith` takes the whole wrapper: a separate `value: 'a`
   parameter is a borrow in an *argument* position, where there is an owner in the caller's frame to
   point at and no fold, so the instance could not spell it.
-}
pub alias HexFormat = Formatted(HexBytesStyle, [U8])

-- `de:ad:be:ef` is a MAC address, `de ad be ef` a hex dump's row, and `deadbeef` a digest - which is
-- the whole of what the separator is for and why the default is not to write one.
pub fn hexBytesStyle(upper: Bool = False, separator: CodeUnit = 0) -> HexBytesStyle =
    HexBytesStyle {upper: upper, separator: separator}

pub fn hexBytes(bytes: '[U8], upper: Bool = False, separator: CodeUnit = 0) -> HexFormat =
    Formatted {value: bytes, style: hexBytesStyle(upper, separator)}

-- The reader, named rather than ascribed - `Core/Read.yana`'s typed set, one entry. `bytesOfHex` is
-- the whole-string form and is `Core/Format.yana`'s generic `readAll` now, since "did the cursor
-- reach the end" is not a question about hexadecimal.
pub fn readHexBytes(from: String, &at: Size, separator: CodeUnit = 0) -> Result(ReadError, [U8]) =
    readWith(hexBytesStyle(separator: separator), from, at)

instance ShowWith(HexBytesStyle, [U8]):
    fn showWith(subject: HexFormat, &to: String):
        let value = subject.style :: HexBytesStyle
        -- The plain lower-case run with nothing between it is what `pushHex` already is, and it is a
        -- vector loop natively. Nothing below it is, so the settings are worth this test.
        if !value.upper && value.separator == 0:
            pushHex(subject.value, to)
            return

        let bytes = subject.value
        let count = length(bytes)
        for i in 0 .. count:
            if i > 0 && value.separator != 0 then String.pushUnit(to, value.separator)

            let byte = bytes[i] :: Int
            String.pushUnit(to, hexUnitCased(byte `shr` 4, value.upper))
            String.pushUnit(to, hexUnitCased(byte `and` 15, value.upper))

    -- Two units a byte, and one separator between each pair of them - so `n + n` plus `n - 1`, which
    -- is exact rather than generous at every setting. A runtime value, like `Show(String)`'s.
    fn showBoundWith(subject: HexFormat) -> Maybe(Size):
        let value = subject.style :: HexBytesStyle
        let count = length(subject.value)
        if count <= (0 :: Size) then return Just(0)

        return Just(count + count + (if value.separator != 0 then count - 1 else 0))

-- `hexUnit` with the other alphabet available. The digits below ten are the same units either way.
pub fn hexUnitCased(nibble: Int, upper: Bool) -> CodeUnit
  | {<10, _}   -> truncate("0" + nibble)
  | {_,  True} -> truncate(nibble - 10 + "A")
  | _          -> truncate(nibble - 10 + "a")

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Reading a run of bytes back
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   The dual of `hexBytes` and of `hexOf`, and the operation every fingerprint typed into a config
   file, every `.sha256` sidecar and every hex dump being read back needs. `hexValue` above is the
   digit half of it and has been here all along; this is the run.

   **The result is owned**, which is what a reader can answer and a formatter cannot: there is no
   subject to borrow, so the bytes are a new `Array(U8)` and the caller has it outright.

   **Two digits make a byte and one does not.** A run stops before an odd trailing digit rather than
   consuming it or padding it, so `readHexBytes` on `abc` reads one byte and leaves the cursor on the
   `c` - which is the answer that lets a caller tell the two apart. A run of no bytes at all is
   `NoDigits`, on `Core/Read.yana`'s terms: nothing was consumed and the cursor did not move.

   The separator is the one the writer writes - the same `HexBytesStyle`, which is what makes the
   pair a round trip - and it counts only *between* bytes for the reason `Format/Number.yana`'s group
   separator does, so `de:ad:` reads two bytes and leaves the colon.

   **The subject differs between the directions, and the two classes are why that is allowed.**
   Writing takes a `Flat(U8)` window because the bytes are already somewhere; reading answers an
   owned `Array(U8)` because there is nothing to borrow. One style, two subjects, and no class that
   has to pretend they are the same type.

   `upper` is written and not read: `hexValue` takes either alphabet, and a reader that refused `FF`
   because its style said lower case would be describing the text rather than reading it.
-}
instance ReadWith(HexBytesStyle, [U8]):
  fn readWith(style: HexBytesStyle, from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, [U8]):
    let separator = style.separator
    let units = length(from)
    let &->bytes = emptyArray() :: [U8]
    let &start = at

    while start + 1 < units:
        -- A separator is expected before every byte but the first, and its absence ends the run
        -- rather than failing it: what follows a digest in a line is the caller's to read.
        let &cursor = start
        if separator != 0 && length(bytes) > 0:
            if String.unit(from, cursor) != separator then break
            cursor = cursor + 1

        if cursor + 1 >= units then break

        let high = hexValue(String.unit(from, cursor))
        let low = hexValue(String.unit(from, cursor + 1))

        match {high, low}:
            {Just(->upper), Just(->lower)}:
                push(bytes, truncate((upper `shl` 4) `or` lower) :: U8)
                start = cursor + 2
            _ -> break

    if length(bytes) == 0 then return Err(NoDigits)

    at = start
    return Ok(bytes)
